{
  "id": "plain-data-retention-policy",
  "set": "plain",
  "kind": "document",
  "language": "en",
  "title": "Data retention policy",
  "headings": [
    "Purpose",
    "What we keep and for how long",
    "Deletion",
    "Exceptions"
  ],
  "path": null,
  "text": "# Purpose\n\nThis policy sets how long the company keeps each kind of record and how records are deleted when that time ends. Keeping records longer than needed raises cost and risk, and deleting them too early can break a legal duty. Every team that stores records follows this policy, and the data protection lead reviews it once a year.\n\n# What we keep and for how long\n\nApplication logs are kept for ninety days, then deleted. Backups of the production database are kept for thirty-five days. Customer content is kept while the account is open and for thirty days after it closes, so a customer who closes an account by mistake can get it back. Invoices and other accounting records are kept for the period the tax rules require. Support tickets are kept for two years after they close. Recordings of sales calls are kept for one year, and only where the customer agreed to the recording at the start of the call.\n\n# Deletion\n\nDeletion is automatic wherever the system allows it. Each storage system has a scheduled job that removes records past their retention period, and the job writes a count of what it removed to the audit log. Where deletion is manual, the owner of the system deletes the records at the start of each month and records the count in the same log. Backups past their period are destroyed, not archived.\n\n# Exceptions\n\nA legal hold suspends deletion for the records it names, until legal lifts it. A customer's request to delete their content is carried out within thirty days, even if the retention period has not ended, unless a legal hold applies. Any other exception needs written approval from the data protection lead, with an end date, and is listed in the register of exceptions.\n",
